IMPORTANT RULES:Do NOT rewrite the whole projectGive only minimal file changesMention exact filenamesExplain WHY the architecture mattersTeach like a senior engineer mentoring a beginnerKeep explanations beginner-friendlyDo not overwhelm me with too much code at onceShow moreMay 29Claude responded: Good.Good. Clean base. Let's organize.

Step 1: Create folders first
In frontend/src/, create these folders:
powershellcd frontend/src
mkdir pages
mkdir components
mkdir router
Why?

pages = full screens (HomePage, LoginPage)
components = reusable pieces (Button, Navbar)
router = all navigation logic in one place

Rule: pages use components. App.jsx uses router. Nothing else.

Step 2: Create HomePage
File: frontend/src/pages/HomePage.jsx
jsxfunction HomePage() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome to CollabSpace</h1>
    </div>
  )
}

export default HomePage
Why separate file?

App.jsx should never contain UI. It's a container, not a page.

Step 3: Create Router
File: frontend/src/router/index.jsx
jsximport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom'
import HomePage from '../pages/HomePage'

function AppRouter() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<HomePage />} />
      </Routes>
    </BrowserRouter>
  )
}

export default AppRouter
Why separate router file?

When you have 10 pages later, App.jsx stays clean. All routes live in one place.

Step 4: Update App.jsx
Only change — replace everything with this:
jsximport AppRouter from './router/index'

function App() {
  return <AppRouter />
}

export default App
Why? App.jsx now does ONE job — render the router. Nothing else.

Try it yourself ✋

Make those 3 folders
Create those 3 files
Run npm run dev
Check http://localhost:5173 still works

