You tell me — what data do we need to fetch when this page loads? 🧱You said: everything in prooper order in columnnseverything in prooper order in columnns11:38 PMClaude responded: Yes.Yes. More specific:

Fetch all tasks for a workspace → then filter by status into 3 columns.

tasks where status = "todo"        → TODO column
tasks where status = "in_progress" → IN PROGRESS column  
tasks where status = "done"        → DONE column

Here's the KanbanPage code. Read it first:
jsximport { useState, useEffect } from "react"
import api from "../api/axios"

function KanbanPage() {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([])

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchTasks()
  }, [])

  const fetchTasks = async () => {
    const token = localStorage.getItem("token")
    try {
      const response = await api.get("/tasks/1", {
        headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }
      })
      setTasks(response.data)
    } catch (error) {
      alert("Failed to load tasks")
    }
  }

  const todo = tasks.filter(t => t.status === "todo")
  const inProgress = tasks.filter(t => t.status === "in_progress")
  const done = tasks.filter(t => t.status === "done")

  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-6">Kanban Board</h1>
      <div className="grid grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        
        <div className="bg-gray-100 p-4 rounded-lg">
          <h2 className="font-bold text-lg mb-3">TODO</h2>
          {todo.map(task => (
            <div key={task.id} className="bg-white p-3 rounded shadow mb-2">
              <p className="font-semibold">{task.title}</p>
              <p className="text-gray-500 text-sm">{task.description}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div className="bg-gray-100 p-4 rounded-lg">
          <h2 className="font-bold text-lg mb-3">IN PROGRESS</h2>
          {inProgress.map(task => (
            <div key={task.id} className="bg-white p-3 rounded shadow mb-2">
              <p className="font-semibold">{task.title}</p>
              <p className="text-gray-500 text-sm">{task.description}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div className="bg-gray-100 p-4 rounded-lg">
          <h2 className="font-bold text-lg mb-3">DONE</h2>
          {done.map(task => (
            <div key={task.id} className="bg-white p-3 rounded shadow mb-2">
              <p className="font-semibold">{task.title}</p>
              <p className="text-gray-500 text-sm">{task.description}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  )
}

export default KanbanPage
You tell me — what does tasks.filter() do? 🧱You said: it filters in which column it should be init filters in which column it should be in11:39 PMClaude responded: Exactly.Exactly. 💪

filter() loops through all tasks → keeps only ones matching the condition → returns new array.

So tasks.filter(t => t.status === "todo") → gives only todo tasks.

